{"id": "39419bcd-621a-4cf8-a9d0-a1a74bf928a4", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The user already suspects EBS IO burst balance depletion on a burstable instance, so this isn't an open-ended mystery—it's a targeted verification task: confirm the instance type, check EBS-optimized throughput limits, review volume configuration, and pull the relevant metrics to validate that hypothesis.

Since checking metrics across multiple calls would be token-heavy, I'll delegate this to a sub-agent with metrics-exploration skills preloaded, using a baseline window starting a couple hours before the current time. I'll lay out the plan for the user first, then hand it off.

"}]}

{"id": "e23b04d2-bae5-44ff-923b-ebcf5e500786", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll check this against what the instance can actually deliver. That means comparing the instance type's EBS baseline and burst limits with the gp3 volume's provisioned IOPS and throughput, then looking at the EBS IO balance and the volume's actual I/O over the last couple of hours.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_KFuFsv4yGzrH1GoD0SJ2D8", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-io-balance-check", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Determine whether EBS-level throttling from a burstable instance's EBS bandwidth/IOPS limits (EBS IO balance depletion) explains the slowness of the application on instance i-0123456789abcdef0_1, and quantify the mismatch between the gp3 volume's provisioned performance and what the instance can deliver.",
  "context": "User reports the application on this burstable instance has become slow over the last ~30 minutes. They state that gp3 volume vol-0123456789abcdef0 is provisioned above what the instance can deliver and that EBSIOBalance% is draining. Validate (do not just assume) these claims. This is a bounded, shallow check, not a full root-cause hunt. Read-only only. Agent space account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1 (check the instance's actual account/region via the available associations if not found there).",
  "resources": ["i-0123456789abcdef0_1", "vol-0123456789abcdef0"],
  "timeframe": "Incident window: 2026-11-11T18:30:00Z to 2026-11-11T19:03:00Z. Baseline: 2026-11-11T16:30:00Z to 2026-11-11T18:30:00Z.",
  "criteria": "1) Instance type, EBS-optimized status, and the instance type's EBS baseline/maximum IOPS and throughput (baseline vs burst, from describe_instance_types EbsInfo) vs the volume's provisioned IOPS/throughput/size/attachments (describe_volumes). 2) CloudWatch AWS/EC2 EBSIOBalance% and EBSByteBalance% for the instance, plus EBSReadOps/EBSWriteOps/EBSReadBytes/EBSWriteBytes (convert to IOPS and MB/s) in incident vs baseline windows; note when balance began draining and whether it hit 0. 3) AWS/EBS volume metrics for the volume (VolumeReadOps, VolumeWriteOps, VolumeReadBytes, VolumeWriteBytes, VolumeQueueLength, VolumeTotalReadTime/WriteTime, VolumeIdleTime, and any VolumeThroughputPercentage/ExceededIOPS/ExceededTP metrics available) to check for queueing/latency increase. 4) Also check CPUUtilization and CPUCreditBalance for the instance to rule in/out CPU credit exhaustion as a concurrent or alternative cause. Report concise findings: what is confirmed, what is not, the timeline, and numbers (actual IOPS/throughput vs instance limits vs volume provisioned). Suggest remediation options (e.g., larger instance type, lower provisioned volume performance to match, etc.) but do not perform any changes."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Check EBS IO balance, volume and instance limits for the slow instance"}], "created_at": null}

